    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "idk i guess. it just feels like there is a lot of liability there. like what if there is a dispute at the very last minute and everyone is just fighting over who gets what? how does the agent decide who is right if the seller says they did everything but i think they didnt? it seems like a lot of power for one person to have over the funds.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Disputes can certainly occur in real estate transactions, which is why the role of the escrow agent is so crucial. They're there to remain neutral and follow the terms of the contract closely. In the event of a dispute, the escrow agent typically withholds the funds until the disagreement is resolved, sometimes with the intervention of a third-party mediator or legal process, until the contract's conditions have been satisfactorily met by both parties.\n\nThe escrow agent's responsibility is to enforce the contract as written, and if the conditions for releasing funds are not met, the funds remain in escrow. Their job is to facilitate a transparent, fair, and balanced process, thus they are empowered to make decisions based on the agreement in place and any relevant laws. They're often paired with other professionals, such as real estate attorneys, to provide guidance and resolve any issues that arise.\n\nIf either party believes there is an issue with how the escrow agent is handling the situation, they can usually contact the agent to address concerns or seek advice. In the rare case where an escrow agent has not acted in good faith, there are legal avenues to challenge their actions. It’s, as overwhelming as it might seem, a system designed to ensure everyone is held to the same rules and that",
      "source": "target",
